/**
  @page guides-solid-mechanics Solid mechanics
  @brief Kinematics, constitutive laws and Newton solution for finite-strain solid mechanics.

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-solid-intro Introduction

  The @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid" module implements finite-strain
  (hyperelastic) solid mechanics on top of the variational form language.
  Its modules separate local constitutive response, finite element assembly
  and output fields:

  | Layer | Directory | Contents |
  |-------|-----------|----------|
  | Kinematics | `Solid/Kinematics/` | Deformation measures and invariants at a point |
  | Constitutive laws | `Solid/Constitutive/` | Strain-energy densities and their derivatives |
  | Integrators | `Solid/Integrators/` | The internal virtual work residual and tangent |
  | Point plumbing | `Solid/Local/` | Constitutive-point inputs (state, fibers, activation) |
  | Postprocessing | `Solid/Fields/` | Stress and strain fields for output |
  | Linear theory | `Solid/Linear/` | Small-strain elasticity integrators |

  Working examples are available in
  [BlockGravity.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/BlockGravity.cpp)
  (quasi-static NeoHookean block under ramped gravity),
  [CantileverBeam.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/CantileverBeam.cpp),
  and [ActiveContractionPlaneWave.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/ActiveContractionPlaneWave.cpp)
  (active fiber contraction driven by an activation wave).

  The residual/tangent sign convention and finite-difference consistency
  checks used by Solid are part of Rodin's shared
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "Numerical contracts".

  @section guides-solid-kinematics Kinematics

  With displacement @f$ u @f$, the deformation gradient is
  @f$ F = I + \nabla_X u @f$, the right Cauchy–Green tensor
  @f$ C = F^\top F @f$, and the Green–Lagrange strain
  @f$ E = \tfrac12 (C - I) @f$. Isotropic response is expressed through
  the invariants @f$ I_1 = \operatorname{tr} C @f$, @f$ I_2 @f$,
  @f$ I_3 = \det C = J^2 @f$; a fiber direction @f$ f_0 @f$ adds the
  anisotropic invariant @f$ I_{4f} = f_0 \cdot C f_0 @f$ (squared fiber
  stretch).

  These live in `Solid/Kinematics/` (`KinematicState`, `Invariants`) and
  are computed *at constitutive points* — laws never see meshes or
  quadrature, only local state.

  `InternalVirtualWork` uses the total Lagrangian formulation with integration
  over the reference configuration, reference gradients @f$\nabla_X@f$
  and first Piola–Kirchhoff stress @f$P@f$. The Cauchy stress is available
  as an output field through @ref guides-solid-fields.

  @section guides-solid-laws Constitutive laws

  A hyperelastic law is a strain-energy density @f$ W @f$; stress and
  tangent follow by differentiation.
  @f$ P = \partial W / \partial F @f$,
  @f$ \mathbb{C} = \partial^2 W / \partial F^2 @f$. All laws derive from
  `HyperElasticLaw` and return, at a constitutive point, the stress and
  the *consistent* tangent.

  | Law | Energy | Regime / notes |
  |-----|--------|----------------|
  | `Hooke` | quadratic in @f$ \varepsilon @f$ | Linear elasticity |
  | `SaintVenantKirchhoff` | quadratic in @f$ E @f$ | Large rotations, small strains; loses ellipticity in strong compression |
  | `NeoHookean` | @f$ I_1 @f$-based, compressible | General-purpose rubber-like |
  | `MooneyRivlin` | @f$ I_1, I_2 @f$-based | Rubber with second-invariant sensitivity |
  | `HolzapfelOgden` | isotropic matrix + exponential fiber term in @f$ I_{4f} @f$ | Anisotropic soft tissue, one fiber family |
  | `ActiveFiberLaw` | 1D fiber element with internal state | Active (muscle) contraction |
  | `ActiveContraction<Passive, Active>` | passive law + active fiber law | Composition wrapper |

  The [BlockGravity.cpp](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/BlockGravity.cpp)
  example constructs a law and its internal virtual work form as follows.

  @code{.cpp}
  Solid::NeoHookean law(lambda, mu);
  auto ivw = Solid::InternalVirtualWork(law, u);   // u: current displacement GridFunction
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-solid-response Check a local material response

  The `ConstitutiveResponse` example evaluates a three-dimensional compressible
  Neo-Hookean law along @f$F=\operatorname{diag}(s,1,1)@f$, with Lamé
  coefficients @f$\lambda=2@f$ and @f$\mu=1@f$ in the chosen stress units.
  The other two stretches are held at one. All sampled determinants are
  positive, with @f$0.7\le s\le1.4@f$.

  @snippet Solid/ConstitutiveResponse.cpp constitutive-bind

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html ConstitutiveResponse-energy.png "Stored energy evaluated from the constitutive law."
  @image html ConstitutiveResponse-stress.png "First Piola stress compared with the finite-difference energy derivative."
  @endparblock

  Along this loading path, @f$dW/ds=P_{11}@f$. The program checks that identity
  using centered differences with increment @f$10^{-6}@f$. The maximum scaled
  discrepancy in the checked run is @f$1.92\times10^{-10}@f$, below the
  program's @f$10^{-8}@f$ threshold. This checks the local energy/stress
  relation; finite element equilibrium and residual/tangent consistency are
  separate checks.

  @section guides-solid-newton The weak form and Newton's method

  Static equilibrium in weak form is

  @f[
    R(u; v) = \int_{\Omega_0} P(F) : \nabla_X v \; dX - \ell_{ext}(v) = 0 ,
  @f]

  and Newton's method solves, at each iterate,
  @f$ R'(u)(\delta u, v) = -R(u; v) @f$. The tangent
  @f$ R' @f$ contains both the material stiffness (from
  @f$ \partial^2 W/\partial C^2 @f$) and the geometric (initial-stress)
  stiffness; omitting the latter forfeits quadratic convergence.
  `InternalVirtualWork` packages residual and tangent together — the
  expression `ivw(du, v)` contributes both to the Newton problem.

  @code{.cpp}
  // Newton linearization:  K δu = -F_int(u) + F_ext
  Problem newton(du, v);
  newton = ivw(du, v)
         - Integral(bodyForce, v)
         + DirichletBC(du, zero).on(bottomBC);

  SparseLU linearSolver(newton);
  NewtonSolver solver(linearSolver);
  solver.setMaxIterations(50)
        .setAbsoluteTolerance(1e-10)
        .setRelativeTolerance(1e-8);
  solver.solve(u);      // iterates du-solves, accumulating into u
  @endcode

  The *increment* @f$ \delta u @f$ is the trial
  function; the Dirichlet condition on @f$ \delta u @f$ is homogeneous
  once @f$ u @f$ satisfies the constraint; loads are ramped in steps
  (incremental loading) because a full load applied to the undeformed
  state may lie outside Newton's basin of attraction.

  New solid terms should be documented and tested as residual/tangent pairs;
  the tangent must be the derivative of the residual assembled into the Newton
  problem.

  @section guides-solid-inputs Constitutive-point inputs

  Laws that need more than kinematics (time step, activation, previous
  internal state, fiber directions) receive them through the
  *constitutive point* mechanism
  (<a href="_constitutive_point_8h.html">Solid/Local/ConstitutivePoint.h</a>).
  The integrators stamp tags — cell index, quadrature-point index, and any
  user-relevant quantities — onto the point, and a user-supplied input callable
  maps tags to law inputs.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto ivw = Solid::InternalVirtualWork(law, u).setInput(activeInput);
  @endcode

  Direct constitutive evaluation binds inputs once, then reads the prepared
  law through const methods.

  @code{.cpp}
  Solid::KinematicState state(3);
  state.setDisplacementGradient(gradient);
  Solid::ConstitutivePoint point(state);
  Solid::NeoHookean law(lambda, mu);
  law.setConstitutivePoint(point);
  Math::SpatialMatrix<Real> stress, increment;
  law.getFirstPiolaKirchhoffStress(stress);
  law.getMaterialTangent(increment, deformationIncrement);
  const Real energy = law.getStrainEnergyDensity();
  @endcode

  The law owns its kinematic snapshot and keeps intermediate data private.
  Changing a point or its borrowed state affects evaluation only after the
  next non-const @c setConstitutivePoint() call. Binding compares the consumed
  kinematics and auxiliary inputs exactly and reuses unchanged data; no
  address or cache identity determines validity. Spatially graded laws sample
  their coefficient on each binding. Copies have independent prepared state,
  and assembly uses separate working laws per worker. The same law must not
  be rebound concurrently without external synchronization; const reads of
  an already prepared law perform no cache updates.

  An output supplied to @c InternalVirtualWork::setOutput() receives the
  constitutive point and a const prepared law, from which it can read stresses,
  energy or active residuals. Both references are valid for the callback;
  retain a law copy if its prepared response is needed afterwards.

  This keeps laws pure functions of local state (unit-testable against
  finite differences at a single point) and keeps integrators ignorant of
  constitutive details. <a href="_fiber_kinematics_8h.html">Solid/Local/FiberKinematics.h</a>
  carries preferred directions; fibers are *material* (reference-configuration)
  vectors —
  their pushforward @f$ F f_0 @f$ happens inside laws and fields, never in
  user setup.

  @subsection guides-solid-internal Internal variables

  Active laws evolve internal state (active extension, cross-bridge
  stiffness-like variables) by local ODEs. Their numerical treatment has two requirements:

  - The preferred architecture for internal variables in Rodin is to make
    them **first-class fields** (grid functions on discontinuous spaces,
    coupled into the global Newton residual). Their evolution and consistent
  linearization are then represented in the coupled system.
  - Relaxation after deactivation depends on the internal-state evolution,
    constitutive assumptions and mechanical boundary conditions. Report the
    active state, relevant time scales, loads and equilibrium residual when
    assessing relaxation. Dynamic models also include their specified inertia
    and time-integration scheme.

  @section guides-solid-fields Postprocessing fields

  `Solid/Fields/` provides stress/strain functions for output and
  coupling through `FirstPiolaKirchhoffStress` (@f$ P @f$),
  `CauchyStress` (@f$ \sigma = J^{-1} P F^\top @f$),
  `GreenLagrangeStrain` (@f$ E @f$). They are ordinary
  @ref Rodin::Variational "form-language" functions. Project them onto an
  output space and write them with @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF".

  @section guides-solid-units A note on units

  Select and state one unit system near the example parameters. Moduli,
  stresses, tractions and body-force densities must use the corresponding
  force and length units. Time-dependent laws also require consistent time
  and rate parameters.

  @section guides-solid-seealso See also

  - @ref guides-variational — problem composition
  - @ref guides-solvers — the Newton solver and linear solvers
  - @ref Solid/ConstitutiveResponse.cpp "Checked constitutive response source"
  - @ref Rodin::Heart "Heart" — 0D reduced cardiac models for calibrating
    the 3D laws
  @example Solid/ConstitutiveResponse.cpp
*/
